Full rewind: rewound to x92 (start) from 920.7, energy 24.1, worldTime 0.33, echo 4. Good. 186 frames = 3.1s for 5s. Works.

Crumble long rewind from death: state 0, y620 (spawn! not the crumble position? Because rewind from death to earliest snapshot, player at spawn), dead false, invuln. It resurrects at spawn but state restored. Maybe rewinding all the way to the beginning is fine. Hist 2 (one snapshot + post-rewind). Good.

Moving platform restore: x436.6 → 358 after full rewind, matching worldTime 0. Good.

So rewind works well.

One UX issue: Full rewind after death brought the player all the way to spawn (x92) instead of the last safe point before death. That's because the history included standing at spawn for 2.7s, then walking; full 5s. Fine. If the player holds rewind, they rewind to earliest. That's intended.

Now, test pause/menu navigation/save/settings, sector select. Also the "Continue" card after progress and resume. Let's write a UI test.

But first make the code fixes, then test everything.

### Implement fixes

1. favicon in index.html.
2. CSS:
   - `body.touch.is-playing .touch-controls` instead of body.touch.
   - logo sizes in portrait.
   - core panel nowrap, portrait widths.
   - `only-touch` / `only-desktop` classes.
   - maybe hide footer keyboard hints on touch.
3. ui.js:
   - remove inline display style in `_detectTouch`; perhaps set `this.touch.style.display` not needed. But if CSS uses `.is-playing`, add class toggling in game loop.
   - Adapt energy hint based on touch.
4. game.js: toggle `is-playing` class each frame (or on mode changes).
   Better performance: only when changed. Add in loop:
```
const playingClass = this.mode === 'playing';
if (playingClass !== this._wasPlaying) { document.body.classList.toggle('is-playing', playingClass); this._wasPlaying = playingClass; }
```
Initialize `this._wasPlaying = false` in constructor. Or simply `classList.toggle` each frame is cheap. Use toggle.
5. index.html death action with spans.
6. draw.js echo visibility.
7. Maybe improve touch controls in portrait: reduce reserved 184px? It's okay. But 184px is 22% of screen height. If we reduce to 150px and shrink buttons, the game view gets taller. Currently the game canvas is 660px tall at 844. That's fine and touch controls are comfortable.

Also, `body.touch .hud { padding-bottom: 150px; }` while portrait sets 188px. But the HUD is positioned over the canvas, which is only 660px tall. 188px bottom padding pushes the energy panel up 188px from canvas bottom, i.e., around y472, which is high above the touch controls (which start at 660). In the screenshot, the energy panel is at y~1100 device = 550 css, canvas bottom 660, so padding ~110? Hmm. Maybe CSS `#game` height is calc(100% - 184px) but HUD is absolute over the app? `.hud { position:absolute; inset:0; }` relative to #app? If the HUD spans full 844, then padding-bottom 188 means energy panel at 656, matching screenshot. The HUD is over the touch control area but padded. Fine. The screenshot shows HUD energy panel just above the touch controls. Good.

Now, hide touch controls on menu: if we remove inline display, the touch controls also disappear from HUD? They are separate. Fine.

### Echo visibility

Modify draw.js inactive echo section. Current:
